 degrees granted non-faculty  researchers and postdocs  and community impact however it also  defines additional metrics  in the existing core areas and includes  new core goals  as well top tier 2.0 is a  forward-looking roadmap  that our university will continue to  refine and follow  over the next decade with respect to  student achievement  we've made market progress over the last  several years  but we need to continue to press onward  and keep aiming high  notably i would like to see our current  six-year graduation rate  increase to 60 percent by 2030.  it's a 16-point jump from our current  level to help get us there  we'll launch a student success  initiative that includes our ongoing  efforts to improve our advisor to  student ratio  from one advisor for each 515 students  to 1 in 350.  we'll also do some national benchmarking  defining best practices  and host a university-wide summit to get  everyone engaged on this critical goal  student success will become an element  of relevant  leadership evaluations i should note  that graduate student success  is integral to this expanded core goal  we need to bolster research scholarship  and creativity  to one include multiple inner and  cross-disciplinary programs  two create more interdisciplinary  centers and institutes  and three increase research and research  productivity  and beyond and lastly four provide  student research opportunities  at all levels at this time i want to  take a moment to highlight a couple of  research faculty  from the college of sciences who were  included in  science magazine's top 10 breakthroughs  of all of 2020.  physicist ashkan salamat's work on room  temperature  super connectivity and astrophysicist  bing zhang's  observation of fast radio bursts or  powerful radio waves coming from deep  space  in fact governor steve sisselak  mentioned ash's superconductivity work  in a state of the state address last  week and referenced how it translates to  energy efficiency  and economic diversification i can  honestly say  my research has never been called out by  a governor in a state of the state  address  wow ash that's really impressive  congratulations to you  and bing for your contributions to the  science community  and for strengthening the unlv brand  the academic health center also has been  a core goal outlined  in the original top tier strategic plan  and will build  a stronger structure to support its  development  as well as a process to identify  timelines milestones  and major benchmarks over the next 20  years to meet our long-term vision  the academic health center is not a  building  but a strategy that integrates and  leverages  the multiple health science disciplines  at unlv to provide  high value and high quality care for the  community  and medical research opportunities it  will also provide a key source of  economic diversity  for our economy we must continue to  foster  community partnerships and that includes  strengthening and expanding our  relationships with the clark county  school district for k-12  and with csn if first-time freshmen or  transfer students are better prepared  to have stronger skills when entering  unlv  they'll make the transition more easy  have greater success  one recent initiative in this area is  the rebel summer series  which our college of education  introduced last year  it's a five-week summer learning  opportunity for local students in grades  two through five  that offers web-based courses and  activities in science and literacy  community partnerships must also include  civic engagement  our community needs to help address the  homeless issue and food insecurity  that many including some of our students  experience  the work of the greenspun college of  urban affairs with the las vegas  sands and nevada partnership for  homeless youth to conduct research and  develop policy  addressing this issue is a perfect  illustration of how we can make a  difference  faculty and students in social work  criminal justice public policy and  leadership  and communication studies are all  involved in carrying out this program  similar efforts are occurring with the  city and county as well  and now for the new core goals in top  tier 2.0  economic development will have a more  front and center role  will pull economic development from its  previous shared pathway goal with  community partnerships  to distinguish its importance while also  broadening its scope  it will become an independent core goal  to stimulate  socioeconomic development this signifies  that we are engaging in these efforts  for all people  in all sectors in all classes and in all  neighborhoods  socioeconomic development includes a  focus on the research park  strengthening industry engagement and  advancing  entrepreneurship and innovation faculty  and students must be active participants  for us to move forward on this goal  because strong universities are vital to  the economic recovery  development and sustainability of  southern nevada  this is our home and i've seen the  commitment you all have to help it  thrive  the other new core goal is to promote  and support  a culture equity and inclusion  previously our top tier values have  alluded to these but bringing this focus  to the forefront  and further defining expectations will  create actionable items  we will cultivate an environment that is  inclusive  welcoming and supportive for all by  assessing and developing conditions  necessary for improvement  that includes things like leadership  accountability and assessment  programming to address unintentional  bias in search committees  faculty and staff mentoring programs to  promote success and emphasizing a  culture  and platforms where all faculty staff  and students can be heard well-known  researchers lee bowman and terrence deal  address culture in their work on  reframing organizations  it's perhaps the most difficult area for  an organization to change  and i know that this new goal may  generate some anxiety  maybe even some skepticism however  we need to realize our deficiencies and  acknowledge there's  inherent structural racism at this  university  we're not unique in that regard most  organizations have  some element of it and in most cases  structural racism  is unintentional and it may be something  as mundane as policies and practices  that impact people differently some may  ask  what does this have to do with  graduating students  but if we're going to reach the next  level  we need to realize and understand that  this is a challenge that needs to be  addressed at unlv 2  and as a major public research  university that has one of the most  diverse  undergraduate student bodies in the  country we should be a leader in this  area  it begins with not being afraid to  discuss it and making it a priority  earlier this week the faculty senate had  an honest  difficult conversation about issues with  diversity and inclusion at unlv  i applaud their insight and desire to  make changes  for a better university environment  faculty and staff  i'll be working with them to refine more  definite goals in this area  some of the programming and professional  development opportunities we need to  offer already exist  the unlv college of liberal arts offices  of diversity initiatives  and student diversity and social justice  in partnership with  the anti-defamation league and the  interfaith council of southern nevada  launched a series last spring that  addressed the effects of racism  on public health indigenous and land  justice  and issues of the first amendment unlv  libraries and the college of urban  affairs  also developed a panel discussion about  race-related media coverage  economics health care criminal justice  and education both series continue in  february during black history month  the anti-black racism task force and the  minority serving institution  or msi task force continue to do work in  this area as well  these are important steps forward we  will cultivate and develop more  finally in top tier 2.0 we will continue  to develop our infrastructure  while ensuring shared governance remains  at the heart  of all that we do but we will also  strategically deepen the culture of  philanthropy  and alumni connection to bolster our  financial sustainability  philanthropy is so important to  continued growth  and success as a university because it  provides the ability to support students  launch new ideas and to make already  great programs even better  three of the many recent examples  include a nine million dollar gift  from the san manuel band of mission  indians  to work with our law and hospitality  programs to develop  first of its kind programs in tribal  gaming law  a six million dollar commitment to  advance brain health  through the chambers grundy center for  transformative neuroscience  and a five million dollar gift from  panda express co-founders  andrew and peggy churn for the nation's  academic program in casual restaurant  management  now some of these strategies will  require organizational shifts  first as you may have seen earlier this  month i've elevated the position of  economic development  to vice president and that function will  be separated out  from the research division now is the  time to make this move because economic  development and diversification  will be critical for the state and the  region as we emerge from the pandemic  second the position of chief diversity  officer will report directly to me  and i'm reviving the ombuds office both  of these directives are designed to  provide a more defined avenue for  faculty staff and students  to voice their concerns or issues lastly  we will move forward with creating a  vice president for health affairs  that will advance collaboration among  the health sciences schools  and ensure the vision mission operations  and health care services are all  strategically aligned  i realize that this may all sound  aggressive given the times that we're in  one of the things that you'll frequently  hear from me say is that we need to make  a dollar out of 15 cents  i know how difficult the budget  situation has been at times and several  of you have mentioned  that we still haven't fully recovered  from the great recession of 2008.  i spent the better part of three decades  in higher education and i know that  budgets are always a challenge  for public urban research universities  we have been fortunate though  that our enrollment has held fairly  steady during this pandemic  and we must be prepared to capitalize on  and be a part of the economic recovery  our focus for the nevada state  legislature this upcoming session  will be on economic development and how  we can support the state  and our partnerships with the clark  county school district and k-12  education  we also want to reinforce the importance  of the medical education building  and the college of engineering building  i am so appreciative to governor sisolak  for including these capital projects in  its proposed  next biennium it's a bold decision  during unprecedented times i would  expect nothing less from our governor  and we will demonstrate that investing  in unlv  is good for everyone  it's been a busy five months and i can't  stop thinking morning day and night  about all the opportunities at unlv i  came here because i have tremendous  respect for this university  and each one of you but no one  individual can move this university  it takes a collective effort from all of  us and we need to realize that we may  fail at times along the way  but don't be afraid of failure learn  from it  build on it and grow from it i won't get  upset if we fail periodically trying  something new  only if we don't try at all we can't  have the attitude  because we've always done it that way  these times require  more innovative and entrepreneurial  thinking than ever before  now rebels aren't afraid to fail or  create a new path  when one doesn't exist i was drawn to  this university  because i identified with that spirit  that determination  that daring style we are all  unconventional independent tenacious and  resilient  that's a rebel our university our  community our society  has changed we need to be thoughtful  and to evaluate adjust and evolve  the decision announced last week to  formally retire the hay rep mascot  is an example i recognize the different  viewpoints on this topic  but we listened we learned and we made a  thoughtful decision  even if everyone doesn't agree as unlv  continues to rise as a top public  research university  we must do so in a way that represents  all of our students  staff and faculty my aspiration  is for unlv to become a premier research  university that's community focused and  world leading  for people from all socioeconomic groups  and backgrounds  can thrive a university that's caring  but practical a university that hears  all voices  and has a rich culture a university  where ideas  and opportunities in closing  i've learned the power of people can  move mountains change can happen  but it's in your hands we can be more  successful we can graduate more students  we can obtain more grants we can do  better in our neighborhood in our  community  we can make great even better but it  takes  all of us to get there thank you for  your dedication your ideas  and your continued hard work
 

